Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 22:38:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09365/11362] Unwedge milestone .557: prior_failures for
 exp6477 and exp6479

The conductor had refused to activate .557 since 2026-08-19, looping on two HARD
SCOPE_MATCHED_PRIOR_FAILURE violations every two minutes. Both matches are real;
neither prior is evidence against its approach.

exp6477 matched exp5708, which ended "blocked: parse_failures" with
parse_failure_count 47 -- the generator's output could not be parsed, so no
constraint record was produced or evaluated. Transport failure, not refutation.

exp6479 matched exp5789, which never executed: all five gates failed at
conductor_pre_gate, the first because the upstream artifact for exp5788 was not
found. The new task carries no gated_on field, so that dependency is gone rather
than assumed satisfied.

Both carry retire_if_same_verdict: true, so a second identical failure retires
the scope instead of returning next milestone.

WHAT THIS TURNED UP, which matters more than the unwedging. exp5788 is recorded
in research-complete.yaml as "OK (conductor)" with a deliverable that does not
exist, was never committed, and has no add OR delete record in git -- it was
never written. An audit of every completed task with a .json deliverable found
504 unique pairs, 57 missing, and 51 of those recorded "OK (conductor)".
Spot-checked five in git history: none were ever added. So this is false-success
logging, NOT the record-destruction class, and no artifact was lost.

The gate layer behaved correctly throughout: it refused to run exp5789 on a
missing upstream. The completion ledger is what overstates, and every downstream
gate that trusts an "OK" inherits the error -- which is how a task that never ran
blocked a successor for a month.

This commit also carries research-roadmap.yaml, which the conductor rewrote when
it activated .557 from the working tree seconds after the fix was written. That
is the conductor's own activation, not a hand edit.

